      "math_academy_id": "q-15304",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "A circle in the Cartesian plane is given by the equation $8x+{x}^{2}-2y=64-{y}^{2}.$ If the circle is written in the standard form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2},$ then $a+b+r=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the standard form of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+{x}^{2}-2y & =64-{y}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+8x)+({y}^{2}-2y) & =64 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & =64+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={9}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={9}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-4,b=1$ and $r=9,$ and\n\n$$\na+b+r=-4+1+9=6.\n$$",
